Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** example that shows how to:

1. Add a button to the page (or use any existing UI element).  
2. Listen for the click event.  
3. Call `map.flyTo()` with a set of options that give you a smooth, eased transition to the new centre, zoom, bearing and pitch.  
4. (Optional) Cancel an in-flight animation if the user clicks again before the previous flight finishes.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – smooth flyTo on button click</title>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    /* Simple button styling – keep it accessible */
    #flyBtn {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px; left: 10px;
      padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
      background: #fff;
      border: 1px solid #999;
      border-radius: 4px;
      cursor: pointer;
      font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
      font-size: 0.9rem;
    }
    #flyBtn:focus { outline: 2px solid #0066ff; }
  </style>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>
<button id="flyBtn" aria-label="Fly to San Francisco">Fly to SF</button>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (light-vector basemap)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any style you like
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // start over San Francisco
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0,
    // If you are using satellite imagery instead of a vector basemap,
    // remember the cartographic rule from the skill: use a dark halo for
    // any text you add later.
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Define the target view (where we want to fly)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const target = {
    // Longitude, latitude (Web Mercator)
    center: [-122.4194, 37.7749], // San Francisco City Hall (example)
    zoom: 15,                     // a bit closer
    bearing: 30,                  // rotate the map 30° clockwise
    pitch: 45,                    // tilt the view for a 3-D feel
    // The following options control the animation itself
    duration: 2500,               // ms – longer = slower, smoother
    curve: 1.42,                  // default “fly” curve (1.0 = linear)
    easing: t => t,               // linear easing – you can use d3-ease if you like
    // Optional: stop any ongoing animation when a new one starts
    // (MapLibre does this automatically, but you can call map.stop() yourself)
  };

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Hook the button click → flyTo()
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const btn = document.getElementById('flyBtn');

  btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    // If you want to cancel a previous flight instantly:
    // map.stop();

    map.flyTo(target);
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  (Optional)  Keep UI state in sync with the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // Example: disable the button while the animation is running
  map.on('movestart', () => btn.disabled = true);
  map.on('moveend',   () => btn.disabled = false);
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Why this works – the cartographic reasoning behind the options

| Option | What it does | Why you usually want it for a “smooth” fly |
|--------|--------------|--------------------------------------------|
| `center` | Target longitude/latitude | Moves the camera to the exact feature you care about. |
| `zoom`   | Target zoom level | Higher zoom = more detail; the transition will interpolate the scale, which feels natural. |
| `bearing`| Rotation around the vertical axis | Adding a modest bearing (15-45°) gives a sense of motion rather than a flat jump. |
| `pitch`  | Tilt of the camera (0-60° typical) | A slight tilt (30-45°) adds depth and makes the flight feel three-dimensional, especially over terrain or hillshade layers. |
| `duration`| Milliseconds for the whole animation | 1500-3000 ms is a sweet spot: fast enough to keep the UI responsive, slow enough for the eye to follow the motion. |
| `curve`  | Controls the “flight path” curvature (default 1.42) | Larger values make the camera rise higher before descending, which looks more cinematic. |
| `easing` | Function that maps progress (0-1) → eased progress | Linear (`t => t`) is fine, but you can plug in `d3.easeCubicInOut` or similar for a more natural acceleration/deceleration. |
| `map.stop()` (optional) | Cancels any in-flight animation | Prevents a queue of flights if the user clicks repeatedly. |

### Accessibility notes (from the **maplibre-cartography** skill)

* **Keyboard-focusable button** – the `<button>` element is natively focusable; we added a visible `:focus` outline so keyboard users can see when it’s selected.  
* **ARIA label** – `aria-label="Fly to San Francisco"` gives screen-reader users a clear description of the action.  
* **Contrast** – The button’s white background on the default light basemap meets WCAG AA contrast. If you switch to a dark or imagery basemap, change the button’s colors accordingly (e.g., dark background with light text).  
* **Minimum label size** – The button text is 0.9 rem (≈ 14 px on most browsers), which satisfies the “≥ 10 px” rule for map-label legibility.

### Tweaking for other basemap types

* **If you are using satellite/aerial imagery** as the base layer, remember the rule from the skill: any map-generated text (e.g., a custom POI label you add later) should be **white** with a **dark semi-transparent halo** (`rgba(0,0,0,0.75)`). The button itself is UI, not map-rendered text, so it’s unaffected, but keep the same contrast principle in mind for any on-map symbols you later add.  

* **If you have hillshade or terrain layers** underneath the vector data, the `pitch` you choose should roughly match the hillshade illumination direction (commonly 315° NW) so the 3-D feel of the flight aligns with the terrain shading.

---

#### TL;DR code snippet

```js
// Assuming `map` is already created
document.getElementById('flyBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  map.flyTo({
    center: [-122.4194, 37.7749],
    zoom: 15,
    bearing: 30,
    pitch: 45,
    duration: 2500,
    curve: 1.42,
    easing: t => t   // replace with d3.easeCubicInOut if you like
  });
});
```

That’s all you need for a smooth, cartographically-aware camera fly-to after a button click in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!
